Maximum Subarray Min-Product (Hard)
🔹 Problem Statement

Given an integer array nums, return the maximum min-product of any non-empty subarray of nums.

The min-product of a subarray is defined as:

(Minimum element in the subarray) × (Sum of all elements in that subarray)

Since the answer may be large, return it modulo 10⁹ + 7.

🔹 Example 1
Input: nums = [1,2,3,2]
Output: 14
Explanation:

Subarrays:

Subarray	Min	Sum	Min × Sum
[1]	1	1	1
[2]	2	2	4
[3]	3	3	9
[2]	2	2	4
[1,2,3]	1	6	6
[2,3,2]	2	7	14 ✅
[1,2,3,2]	1	8	8

Maximum = 14

🔎 Intuition

If we fix an element nums[i] as the minimum, then:

We must find how far it can expand left and right

Such that it remains the smallest element

Then compute subarray sum using prefix sum

This is similar to:

Largest Rectangle in Histogram

Monotonic Stack pattern

⚙️ Algorithm (Step-by-Step)
Step 1: Compute Prefix Sum

To calculate subarray sum in O(1).

Step 2: Use Monotonic Stack

Maintain increasing stack.

For each element:

Pop when current is smaller

That popped element becomes the minimum

Compute min-product

Step 3:

For popped index:

left = previous smaller index
right = current index - 1

Use prefix sum:

subarraySum = prefix[right+1] - prefix[left+1]

Compute:

minProduct = nums[poppedIndex] * subarraySum

Track maximum.

💻 Code (Java)
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int maxSumMinProduct(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];
        
        // Step 1: Prefix sum
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];
        }
        
        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
        long max = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            
            long curr = (i == n) ? 0 : nums[i];
            
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && nums[stack.peek()] > curr) {
                int minIndex = stack.pop();
                
                int left = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();
                int right = i - 1;
                
                long sum = prefix[right + 1] - prefix[left + 1];
                long product = sum * nums[minIndex];
                
                max = Math.max(max, product);
            }
            
            stack.push(i);
        }
        
        return (int)(max % 1000000007);
    }
}
🧠 Code Explanation (Line by Line Logic)
✅ Prefix Sum
prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];

Allows constant-time subarray sum.

✅ Stack Logic

We maintain increasing order stack.

When:

nums[stack.peek()] > current

We pop because:

Current element limits expansion of popped element.

So popped element's maximum right boundary found.

✅ Calculating Range

If popped index = minIndex

Left boundary:

stack.peek() after pop

Right boundary:

i - 1

Subarray sum:

prefix[right+1] - prefix[left+1]

Multiply by:

nums[minIndex]
⏱ Time Complexity

Prefix sum: O(n)

Each element pushed & popped once: O(n)

✅ Overall: O(n)

